From: mscharged-port
Subject: Rollback: the tracked sounds follow the match as state
Upstream-base: 8b9d92dd88b4c727cc6359ee4cd5ba128cced7b8

A replayed frame starts, stops, pauses and resumes no sound
(MSCHARGED_AUDIO_ACTION, tweak patch 0031), on the idea that its first
run did. With predicted inputs a replay may differ from that first run,
and a sound the replay alone started or stopped was lost. A looping one
then played on: overtime's music, or "Match point"'s (patch 0035), after
the match ended when the goal that ended it came with a late input, until
the hub; a captain's super ability stream (Yoshi's egg), stopped by the
end of the ability that a late input brought, until the same captain's
next one. GameStreams.cpp's functions, which start and stop those, also
returned at once in replays, and their states (sudden death music started,
requested or paused; a captain's stream on) were the audio's, which no
rollback restores.

The tracked sounds (PlayTrackedSound, PlayTrackedOwnedSound: the music, the
captains' chants and streams, the Star) now follow the match as state, as
the audio transitions do (patch 0040). Under the rollback, every start,
stop, pause and resume of one notes, in replays too, what the match wants of
it, by cue and context, in a table among the globals a rollback restores
(Game.cpp); each new start counts in the sound's generation. The audio
keeps what it applied in a table of its own (audio.cpp), which no rollback
takes back, and after every rollback (from the port's rollback, once the
steps a rollback undid ran again: until then their sounds would end and
start again) stops the sounds the match no longer wants, starts from the beginning those
another start replaced, and pauses or resumes the others as the match
wants. A sound that ended by itself keeps its start and is left alone. The
system's pause (PauseAllAudio, ResumeAllAudio, as at a Mega Strike) is not
the match's: it changes neither table, and pauses and resumes wait for its
end. IsSoundTracked answers from the match's table, so that a replay
starting or resuming the stadium's music or a chant decides as the match
wants, not as the audio was left. GameStreams.cpp's functions now run in
replays, their states among the restored globals. When the rollback starts
tracking a match, the sounds that ended by themselves leave both tables, so
that one match after another does not fill them; a full table leaves a
sound to the audio as before.

No caller reads what PlayTrackedSound and PlayTrackedOwnedSound return,
which a replay leaves false. The one-shot sounds (PlaySound, PlayOwnedSound)
are left as they are: one that only a replay plays is still lost. Without
the rollback nothing changes.

